        M = 1e+08 Msun:  r_M =     0.39 kpc,  v_flat =   33.4 km/s,  l0/r_M = 8.062e+07   [(c/v_flat)^2 = 8.062e+07]
        M = 1e+09 Msun:  r_M =     1.22 kpc,  v_flat =   59.4 km/s,  l0/r_M = 2.549e+07   [(c/v_flat)^2 = 2.549e+07]
        M = 1e+10 Msun:  r_M =     3.86 kpc,  v_flat =  105.6 km/s,  l0/r_M = 8.062e+06   [(c/v_flat)^2 = 8.062e+06]
        M = 1e+11 Msun:  r_M =    12.20 kpc,  v_flat =  187.8 km/s,  l0/r_M = 2.549e+06   [(c/v_flat)^2 = 2.549e+06]
        M = 1e+12 Msun:  r_M =    38.59 kpc,  v_flat =  333.9 km/s,  l0/r_M = 8.062e+05   [(c/v_flat)^2 = 8.062e+05]
    alternate:
        M = 1e+08 Msun:  r_M =     0.35 kpc,  v_flat =   35.0 km/s,  l0/r_M = 7.345e+07   [(c/v_flat)^2 = 7.345e+07]
        M = 1e+09 Msun:  r_M =     1.11 kpc,  v_flat =   62.2 km/s,  l0/r_M = 2.323e+07   [(c/v_flat)^2 = 2.323e+07]
        M = 1e+10 Msun:  r_M =     3.52 kpc,  v_flat =  110.6 km/s,  l0/r_M = 7.345e+06   [(c/v_flat)^2 = 7.345e+06]
        M = 1e+11 Msun:  r_M =    11.12 kpc,  v_flat =  196.7 km/s,  l0/r_M = 2.323e+06   [(c/v_flat)^2 = 2.323e+06]
        M = 1e+12 Msun:  r_M =    35.16 kpc,  v_flat =  349.8 km/s,  l0/r_M = 7.345e+05   [(c/v_flat)^2 = 7.345e+05]
  [FAIL] L2 A5's length is the length MOND needs (within a factor 10 of r_M for any real galaxy)   (smallest l0/r_M over 1e8-1e12 Msun and both footings is 7.345e+05; l0/r_M = c^2/sqrt(GMa0) = (c/v_flat)^2 exactly, so the mismatch IS the ratio of the speed of light to a rotation speed, squared)
  [FAIL] L3 a SINGLE fixed filter length can serve the observed galaxy mass range   (the length a linear filter must have to match mass M is proportional to r_M ~ M^1/2, so over 1e8-1e12 Msun the required length spans a factor 100; one operator cannot have 100 lengths)

    A filter could instead take its length from a LOCAL quantity, which is what I4 asks of a
    screening trigger.  Every local length available, at the solar circle (r = 8.2 kpc,
    g = 2.15e-10 m/s^2, |Phi| ~ v^2 = 5.4e10 m^2/s^2, M_b = 6e10 Msun).  A length is only useful
    if it (a) is I4-legal, (b) CARRIES a0 -- otherwise it sets no transition -- and (c) equals r_M:
      canonical: r_M(MW) = 9.45 kpc
        c^2/a0     the A5 operator itself =    3.111e+07 kpc =  3.291e+06 r_M   I4-legal: NO    carries a0: yes
        c^2/g      Rindler, acceleration =    1.355e+07 kpc =  1.433e+06 r_M   I4-legal: yes   carries a0: NO
        g/|grad g| scale-free derivative =          8.2 kpc =  8.675e-01 r_M   I4-legal: yes   carries a0: NO
        |Phi|/a0   the potential         =        18.79 kpc =  1.988e+00 r_M   I4-legal: NO    carries a0: yes
      alternate: r_M(MW) = 8.61 kpc
        c^2/a0     the A5 operator itself =    2.582e+07 kpc =  2.999e+06 r_M   I4-legal: NO    carries a0: yes
        c^2/g      Rindler, acceleration =    1.355e+07 kpc =  1.573e+06 r_M   I4-legal: yes   carries a0: NO
        g/|grad g| scale-free derivative =          8.2 kpc =  9.522e-01 r_M   I4-legal: yes   carries a0: NO
        |Phi|/a0   the potential         =         15.6 kpc =  1.811e+00 r_M   I4-legal: NO    carries a0: yes
  [FAIL] L4 some length that is I4-legal AND carries a0 AND equals r_M exists   (no candidate satisfies all three. c^2/a0 carries a0 but is not local and overshoots by (c/v)^2; c^2/g is local and legal but a0-free, so it sets no transition, and it overshoots by the same (c/v)^2; g/|grad g| is legal but is just the local radius, a0-free and therefore scale-free; |Phi|/a0 hits r_M EXACTLY (v^2 = GM/r_M makes |Phi|/a0 = r_M identically) and is the only one that does -- and it is the potential, which I4 forbids by name and which is not a local invariant)

3. M -- THE MOND GATE on A5-L (the elliptic operator used as the protein)
    sigma(k) = l k^3/(1 + l k) splits exactly: Phi_k = -4 pi G M [ 1/k^2 + 1/(l k^3) ], so the
    point-mass solution is EXACT and elementary,
        Phi(r) = -GM/r + (2GM/(pi l)) ln r + const,      g(r) = GM/r^2 + 2GM/(pi l r).
    The 1/r piece is what A5 buys: the momentum scaling IS changed, exactly as the recipe says.
  [PASS] C2 [control] the inverse transform behind the log term integrates to its closed form (-1)   (quadrature to u = 400 plus the analytic tail gives -1.000000000000 vs exact -1; this integral is the entire content of g_asym = 2GM/(pi l r))
    canonical: exponential-kernel AQUAL point mass, d ln g/d ln M = 0.5030 at 300 kpc (deep MOND) and 1.0000 at 10 pc (Newtonian)
    alternate: exponential-kernel AQUAL point mass, d ln g/d ln M = 0.5028 at 300 kpc (deep MOND) and 1.0000 at 10 pc (Newtonian)
  [PASS] C3 [control] the mass-scaling estimator returns 1/2 on the frozen kernel's own deep-MOND limit   (0.5 asymptotically, 1.0 in the Newtonian regime, both footings)

    A5-L's own mass scaling and amplitude (both footings, at r = 20 kpc and asymptotically):
    canonical: d ln g/d ln M = 1.000000  (MOND needs 0.5);  asymptotic g_A5L/g_MOND = 2.4971e-07  [predicted (2/pi)(v_f/c)^2 = 2.4971e-07]
    alternate: d ln g/d ln M = 1.000000  (MOND needs 0.5);  asymptotic g_A5L/g_MOND = 2.7408e-07  [predicted (2/pi)(v_f/c)^2 = 2.7408e-07]
  [FAIL] M1 A5-L reproduces the MOND mass scaling g ~ M^(1/2) (equivalently the BTFR)   (it returns exactly 1.0000. A filter is a LINEAR operator, so the field equation obeys superposition and g is exactly proportional to M for every f, every l and every symbol. This is independent of the length and kills the linear placement of A5 outright)
  [FAIL] M2 A5-L's asymptotic amplitude matches sqrt(GMa0)/r within a factor 2   (it is short by 4.005e+06 (canonical) and 3.649e+06 (alternate); the deficit is exactly (2/pi)(v_flat/c)^2 and is r-INDEPENDENT, so no radius rescues it)
    canonical: A5-L transition radius (where the two terms balance) = 48.87 Gpc; r_M = 12.20 kpc; ratio 4.005e+06
    alternate: A5-L transition radius (where the two terms balance) = 40.56 Gpc; r_M = 11.12 kpc; ratio 3.649e+06
  [FAIL] M3 A5-L's transition happens at the MOND radius (within a factor 10)   (it happens at pi l0/2 ~ 49 Gpc, past the observable universe; the model is Newtonian everywhere a measurement exists)

    I1 test -- is the effective mu a function of the ACCELERATION?
    canonical: at r = 20 kpc,
        M = 1e+09 Msun: y = g/a0 =   0.0620;  I1 requires mu = 0.060083;  A5-L gives mu_eff = 0.999999591
        M = 1e+11 Msun: y = g/a0 =   0.7232;  I1 requires mu = 0.514798;  A5-L gives mu_eff = 0.999999591
    alternate: at r = 20 kpc,
        M = 1e+09 Msun: y = g/a0 =   0.0564;  I1 requires mu = 0.054815;  A5-L gives mu_eff = 0.999999507
        M = 1e+11 Msun: y = g/a0 =   0.6480;  I1 requires mu = 0.476896;  A5-L gives mu_eff = 0.999999507
  [FAIL] M4 [I1] A5-L's effective mu is a function of y = g/a0, as the frozen kernel requires   (mu_eff = 1/(1 + 2r/(pi l)) depends on r ALONE and is identical (0.999999590752 vs 0.999999590752) for two masses whose required mu differ by 8.6x. A linear operator cannot see an amplitude, so no f produces any mu(y), let alone 1 - e^-y)

    generality over the SYMBOL, not just this f.  For any pure power sigma(k) = l^(a-2) k^a the
    point-mass solution is Phi ~ r^(a-3) (log at a = 3), so g ~ r^(a-4):
        a = 1:  g ~ r^-3   d ln g/d ln M = 1   (-D^2)^-1-type insertion
        a = 2:  g ~ r^-2   d ln g/d ln M = 1   Newton
        a = 3:  g ~ r^-1   d ln g/d ln M = 1   flat rotation curve
        a = 4:  g ~ r^+0   d ln g/d ln M = 1   constant force -- the alpha=1 sunward anomaly
    numerical test of the same statement on an ARBITRARY nonlocal positive-definite operator (200x200,
    dense kernel, not a differential operator): source x100 gives field x100.0000000000, error 1.02e-11
  [FAIL] M5 some elliptic symbol gives BOTH the flat-rotation force law AND a mass scaling other than 1   (a = 3 is the unique exponent with g ~ 1/r (verified exactly by C2 at a = 3 and by Newton at a = 2), and EVERY nonlocal positive-definite operator has d ln g/d ln M = 1 to 1e-11 because the equation is linear in rho. The symbol has one free function and it buys the radial slope only; the mass slope is not for sale at any price)

